From 18471b82119f717cf02bb43afc225257e2770927 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: azrialwork <azrial.work@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 00:46:13 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(cli): enable recursive directory watching on android
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

@parcel/watcher does publish an android-arm64 build — its inotify backend compiled for Bionic, already pinned as a dependency — but patch 0006 stubbed the binding out on the assumption that none exists, so every recursive directory watch (the config directories above all) ended unsupported on android and config changes inside them were only picked up after a restart.

Embed the binding the way the OpenTUI assets are embedded: the CLI build stages @parcel/watcher-android-arm64's watcher.node into a prepared archive, startup extracts it to the cache and re-execs once with OPENCODE_PARCEL_WATCHER_PATH pointing at it, and the android binding loader requires that path. Core's watcher now also maps android to the inotify backend — getBackend() returned undefined for it, which alone marked the platform unsupported. The binding links the libc++_shared.so every Termux install ships, so no compat shim is needed.

Verified on a Redmi Note 9 with the compiled android binary: directory watches start with backend inotify, and an agent file created under .opencode/agent/ appears in the running TUI without a restart.
